{"type":"step-attempt","step":"spec-review","sessionId":"2ad9b8a6-7bb4-414b-8dba-e55fc8559dd8","outcome":{"verdict":"escalation","findingsPath":"specrunner/changes/graph-and-check/spec-review-result-001.md","error":null,"toolResult":{"ok":true,"approved":false,"findings":[{"severity":"high","resolution":"fixable","file":"design.md","title":"C2 raw elements access gap — Graph.elements (Map) loses duplicates before checkC2 can see them","rationale":"Graph.elements is defined as Map<string, Element> (ElementTable). When buildGraph constructs this Map from parsed.elements, any duplicate IDs are silently overwritten. runCheck(graph, manifest, stateKeys?) has no parameter to receive the raw Element[] array, so checker.ts cannot pass the original duplicate-containing array to checkC2(graph, elements: Element[]). C2 (ID uniqueness) cannot be implemented as designed without access to the raw elements."},{"severity":"medium","resolution":"fixable","file":"tasks.md","title":"C1 validateId dependency path violates allowed dependency graph","rationale":"T-06 says 'call validateId' but validateId lives in src/parse/id.ts. Per design/static/dependencies.md, mod-check → mod-parse is NOT an allowed edge (only mod-check → mod-graph is permitted). The design and tasks do not specify whether to re-export validateId through src/graph/, or to reimplement the ID grammar check inline in c01-id-grammar.ts. Without resolution, the implementor either violates the dependency design or duplicates logic without spec guidance."},{"severity":"medium","resolution":"fixable","file":"design.md","title":"VIEW_TYPE_NAMES → prefix mapping unspecified — getEnabledPrefixes cannot convert view type names to prefixes","rationale":"LAYER_MAP is defined as prefix → layer (e.g. uc → views), but manifest.enabled uses human-readable type names like 'use-case', not prefixes. No mapping from enabled name to prefix exists anywhere in design.md or tasks.md. As a result, getEnabledPrefixes (T-05) cannot determine which prefixes to include when a view type is enabled, leaving C3 and C11 enabledPrefixes computation undefined for view types."},{"severity":"medium","resolution":"fixable","file":"design.md","title":"D4 generic rule signature (graph, manifest) is inaccurate for at least 5 rules","rationale":"D4 states the universal rule signature as (graph: Graph, manifest: Manifest) => CheckDiagnostic[], but C2 requires Element[], C6 and C7 take only Manifest, C8 requires stateKeys: string[], and C3 and C11 require enabledPrefixes: Set<string>. The incorrect generic signature is the documentation root-cause that obscures the raw-elements gap in Finding #1."},{"severity":"low","resolution":"fixable","file":"design.md","title":"D2 states Graph bundles 'manifest' but T-02 specifies only manifestPath: string | null","rationale":"D2 describes Graph as bundling manifest as a top-level field alongside ElementTable, ReferenceIndex, etc. T-02's actual Graph type has manifestPath: string | null, not the parsed Manifest object. This inconsistency is misleading for implementors who read the design before the tasks."},{"severity":"low","resolution":"fixable","file":"tasks.md","title":"C3 source-layer filtering behavior for disabled-layer elements is undefined","rationale":"D6 specifies C3 skips references whose target prefix belongs to a disabled layer, but does not address references FROM disabled-layer source elements. For example with enabled: static, a seq-foo element (dynamic, disabled) referencing non-existent mod-nonexistent (static prefix, enabled) would produce a spurious C3 diagnostic, contradicting the 'disabled type obligations are not evaluated' principle. The degradation test (T-19) does not cover this case."}]},"followUpAttempts":1,"transientRetryAttempts":0},"startedAt":"2026-07-02T03:34:23.948Z","endedAt":"2026-07-02T03:42:09.997Z"}
